    "reasoning": "Nitpicks: (1) The agent adds extra context (URL, NDC examples, internal cell references, and an extra note about strength values in Ingredients & Appearance). This is beyond the strict stopping condition but does not materially harm usefulness. (2) Dosage form is summarized as \"tablet, film coated\" rather than quoting the exact Section 3 phrasing (\"Tablets:\" plus detailed descriptions); this is a harmless summarization.\n\nCritical issues: The task explicitly required reporting the Initial U.S. Approval year from the label (and to open relevant label sections rather than infer). The agent reports \"1996\" but the provided screenshots do not show any \"Initial U.S. Approval\" line or year anywhere (screenshots show the label header/metadata, Section 3, and Ingredients & Appearance, but not the approval-year text). Given the evidence set, this approval year is unsupported and could be a hallucination or copied from memory rather than extracted from the label section as required. Because the approval year is one of the required deliverables, this is a critical missing/unsupported element.\n\nWhat is successfully completed: Screenshots do support that the agent searched DailyMed for LIPITOR, selected the correct non-repackaged result with Packager Viatris Specialty LLC, and opened Section 3 showing four strengths (10 mg, 20 mg, 40 mg, 80 mg). Screenshots also support the Ingredients & Appearance fields: Route of Administration ORAL, Ingredient Name ATORVASTATIN CALCIUM TRIHYDRATE (with active moiety info), and Basis of Strength ATORVASTATIN, kept distinct.\n\nOverall outcome: Despite correctly identifying the right label and extracting most required fields, the agent did not demonstrably extract the Initial U.S. Approval year from the label as required (and the evidence contradicts verification). Since the task\u2019s stopping condition requires recording that year along with the other fields, the primary intent is not fully met.",
